# Benchmark build artifacts — regenerated by crisp-compile / crisp-hoist-cuda on every run.
# Do not check these in.

# Hoist byproducts (metadata + per-kernel generated harnesses).
# NOTE: `*_L0.cpp` was missing here until 2026-08-25 while its NVIDIA twin `*_CUDA.cu` was listed,
# so 22 generated Intel harnesses ended up tracked -- they churn on every run and dirtied the tree
# repeatedly.  Same rule, same reason: generated per kernel, regenerated on every sweep.
*.metacrisp
*_CUDA.cu
*_L0.cpp

# Compiled benchmark binaries.  These were tracked by accident (matmul_*_bench_l0, sycl_apples,
# onemkl_optimal, sycl_tla_peer, ...).  Native executables do not belong in source control.
*_bench_l0
matmul_crisp_l0
sycl_apples
sycl_control*
sycl_tla_peer
onemkl_*
cuda_apples
cublas_*
cutlass_peer

# The hand-written fixture harness is SOURCE, not a byproduct, and must stay tracked.  Negated by
# explicit path rather than a blanket `!*.cpp`, which would sit after `*_L0.cpp` above and re-include
# every generated harness it is meant to exclude.
!matmul/crisp/bench_harness_l0.cpp

# Compiled kernels (.ptx / .spv) are regenerable too — but a fallback may be committed for
# machines where crisp-compile is absent, and is re-included by exception below:
#   bench-intel-driver.py  ships the .spv into the oneAPI Docker container
# Everything else is a throwaway artifact.
#
# The four `!matmul/crisp/...` exceptions that used to sit here were removed 2026-08-22: the
# benchmark reorganisation replaced the technique-named chapters with question-named ones and
# `matmul/crisp/` went away with them, so those lines protected files that no longer exist.
# Nothing under matmul/crisp was tracked.  See the note in matmul/README.md if it returns.
*.ptx
*.spv
!reduction/crisp/sum-reduce.spv
